from lib import *
d=docx.Document('JHS_Response_to_Referee.docx')
fill(d,'final count','4,800')
fill(d,'R8: GDP slope',
 'Pooled OLS and random effects without Mundlak means return a single GDP slope (0.380 and 0.387 per pooled SD) that mixes the between-country gradient (0.327 in this panel; 0.326 published) with the much smaller within-country association (0.039; 0.045 published). Two-way country fixed effects, which use only within-country variation, do not establish GDP once errors are clustered by country. In this panel no association is established only between countries, so in that narrow sense country fixed effects miss none; but they lose the GDP gradient and the between-country association of financial confidence, and establish four of the 11 associations CRE establishes. The pooled model fails to establish six of those 11 and reports a positive association for educational-system satisfaction whose within-country association is negative.')
fill(d,'R1.','No predictor is observed in fewer than half of the country-years. The genuinely observed share ranges from 52% (weekly hours worked) to 100%, with a median of 98%; all predictors other than hours worked are observed in at least 86% of country-years, and in 155 to 168 countries.')
fill(d,'R2.','Of the 11 within-country associations established in the country-year baseline (14 in the respondent-level model; see Appendix C, R0), 10 keep sign and significance on observed country-years only; educational-system satisfaction loses significance and none changes sign. The median ratio of observed-only to baseline coefficients is 0.99, and also 0.99 per observed within-country SD, so filled values attenuate slightly rather than inflate the estimates.')
fill(d,'R3.','Because every predictor already exceeds 50% coverage, these thresholds remove no predictor, and all 11 associations keep sign and significance (Appendix Table C3).')
fill(d,'R5.','The results are informative in both directions. With two-way fixed effects and errors clustered by country, the within-country point estimates match the CRE baseline almost exactly, but only four associations remain significant: living standards, food sufficiency, feelings about household income and roads. We now state this sensitivity to the treatment of clustering in Section 4.4. Three of the four (living standards, food sufficiency and feelings about household income) keep sign and significance with country-specific trends and with first differences, with similar magnitudes. The four negative within-country associations (shelter, confidence in financial institutions, air quality and the educational system) move to near zero once country trends are included, which supports the referee’s concern that they reflect shared drift, and the paper now says so. No placebo lead is significant among the 29 predictors (smallest q = .22).')
fill(d,'R6b: result with time-varying',
 'With these time-varying controls, living standards, food sufficiency, feelings about household income, roads, GDP and relative income remain established within countries, whereas the four negative associations and affordable housing lose significance, as they do under country trends.')
fill(d,'R6a.','All four structural within-country associations (food and shelter sufficiency, GDP and relative household income) and the between-country GDP gradient remain established; GDP’s coefficients roughly double because they absorb variation previously shared with perceptual items.')
fill(d,'R6b.','Ladder heaping varies across regions, from 6.2% of answers at 0 or 10 in Europe and Central Asia to 15.0% in Latin America and the Caribbean, while the balanced acquiescence rate varies little (0.47 to 0.53).')
fill(d,'R7.','Under the same linear learner and development-stratified splits, respondent-level R-squared is .120 for GDP alone, .132 for country means only, .001 for within-country deviations only, .146 for undecomposed predictors and .143 for the full level-separated specification (country-year: .599, .661, −.006, .738 and .717). We report plainly that separating levels does not improve prediction: almost all transferable signal comes from between-country differences, and the value of the decomposition is in locating associations, not in predicting better. Section 4.7 now says this.')
fill(d,'R4.','Lowering the threshold to .03 or .04 admits seven items and four further within-country associations, while all 11 baseline associations remain; raising it to .06, .075 and .10 leaves 24, 10 and 6 predictors, and every baseline association still in the model keeps sign and significance.')
d.save('OUT_let.docx'); print(LOG)
